    "q-287667": {
      "dom_id": "step-q287667",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(p-4)x+2=5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4)x+2 & =5 \\\\ 2(p-4)x+2-2 & =5-2 \\\\ {{2(p-4)}_{⏟}}_{a}x & ={{3}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(p-4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(p-4)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ p-4 & =0 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
