    "q-288662": {
      "dom_id": "step-q288662",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n3py=5y+8\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ay=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3py & =5y+8 \\\\ 3py-5y & =5y+8-5y \\\\ 3py-5y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $y$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3p-5)y=8.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ay=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(3p-5)}_{⏟}}_{a}y={{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-5 & =0 \\\\ 3p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 3p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
